'''''Reig v. Ministry of Education''''' (1990–1991) was the first {{w|employment discrimination}} case decided under the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sex-Based Discrimination]] (1986) and the Employment Equality Act (1988) in [[Balboa]]. [[Montserrat Reig i Font]], a teacher in [[Nurra Province|Nurra]], challenged her exclusion from promotion to school principal in favour of a less-qualified male colleague. The [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] ruled in Reig's favour, establishing that the amendment's protections extended to promotion decisions and that the {{w|burden of proof}} in sex-based discrimination cases rested with the employer.

== Background ==

[[Montserrat Reig i Font]] was a secondary school teacher in Nurra Province with fourteen years of teaching experience and a record of consistently strong performance evaluations. In 1989, the Ministry of Education advertised a vacancy for the position of school principal at Reig's school. Reig applied for the position, as did a male colleague who had seven years of teaching experience and had received lower performance evaluations.

The Ministry of Education's regional selection panel awarded the promotion to the male colleague. No formal explanation was provided for the decision, and Reig was not informed of the criteria used in the selection process. Reig requested an explanation from the regional education office but received no response.

== Case ==

Reig brought a case before the [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] in 1990, arguing that the promotion decision constituted {{w|sex discrimination}} under the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sex-Based Discrimination]] (1986) and the Employment Equality Act (1988). Reig's legal team argued that she was better qualified than the successful candidate on every objective measure — experience, performance evaluations, and seniority — and that the only explanation for the decision was her sex.

The Ministry of Education defended the promotion decision on the grounds that the selection panel had considered "overall suitability," which included subjective factors such as "leadership presence" and "community standing." The Ministry argued that these factors were not reducible to objective criteria and that the selection panel's discretion was not subject to review by the Tribunal.

== Ruling ==

The Tribunal ruled in Reig's favour in 1991, ordering her promotion to school principal and {{w|back pay}} for the period during which she should have held the position. The ruling established two significant precedents:

First, the amendment's protections extended to {{w|promotion (rank)|promotion}} decisions, not just hiring and dismissal. The Tribunal held that the exclusion of women from promotion opportunities was a form of discrimination that was functionally equivalent to dismissal, as it limited women's career advancement and earning potential.

Second, the {{w|burden of proof}} in sex-based discrimination cases rested with the employer. The Tribunal held that where a plaintiff could demonstrate that they were better qualified than the successful candidate on objective criteria, the employer was required to demonstrate that the promotion decision was based on objective criteria unrelated to sex. The Ministry of Education's reliance on subjective factors such as "leadership presence" was held to be insufficient, as these factors could not be distinguished from {{w|gender bias}} in the absence of objective measurement.

== Significance ==

The Reig case was the first successful application of the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sex-Based Discrimination]] to an employment discrimination case. The ruling was significant for women in the {{w|public sector]], where promotion structures had historically favoured men, particularly in rural provinces where informal {{w|patronage}} networks reinforced gender hierarchies.

The burden-of-proof precedent established in the Reig case has been cited in subsequent employment discrimination cases and is regarded as one of the most important procedural precedents in Balboan anti-discrimination law. The ruling enabled women to challenge promotion decisions without having to prove the employer's subjective intent, requiring only that they demonstrate superior objective qualifications.

Reig continued to serve as school principal until her retirement in 2005. She became an advocate for women's rights in rural education and was appointed to the advisory board of the Office of Women's Affairs in 1998.

